    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Transcription factors (TFs) are key regulators of cell state changes. Understanding the role of different TFs in regulating cell state transitions can deepen knowledge of an organism’s developmental programs. In plants, numerous TFs have been found to play important roles in differentiation pathways (Schoof et al., 2000).  Because plants have so many TFs (constituting approximately 10% of all genes) (Gonzalez, 2016), screening the function of each TF is difficult. In this thesis, my objective was to develop a screen that determines Zea mays TF function through ectopic expression of a TF gene in maize protoplasts followed by detecting transcriptome changes using an RNA-Seq readout. I used two genes with known roles in the maize anthocyanin pathway, R and C1, to validate the effectiveness of the screen. Additionally, I miniaturized the screen to permit scalability in future studies. Lastly, I tested the feasibility of a T7 expression system to induce high expression levels sufficient for enabling a pooled screen approach.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
